      "writtenOn": "2017-06-22T12:18:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "other crypto-related implementations are using (a somewhat well-defined) Base64 as encoding (e.g. PEM for key and certs, S/MIME ... https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc1421#section-4.3.2.4 ), is there a good reason not re-use it ?\n(a few more characters you can confuse but definitely cut-n-paste safe and not so hard to get right for the usual developer)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 10,
        "endChar": 70
      },
      "revId": "f870a3eb787ecb5feef3853c4c0472643b7a8839",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7660944c_10e0c357",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 10,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000187
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-22T15:41:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If cut-and-paste were an option, Base64 is definitely preferable (standard and 5/6 the size).\n\nBut here, the string is printed on the screen, and the RMA operator has to type it into a different machine - which may be a mobile device with virtual keyboard.  So mixed-case and typos are bigger concerns.",
      "parentUuid": "372def10_055e1a9d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 10,
        "endChar": 70
      },
      "revId": "f870a3eb787ecb5feef3853c4c0472643b7a8839",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
